  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nUpper level trough approaches from west today, bringing increasingly\nupper level diffluent flow and low level convergence which will\nallow for greater coverage of showers/thunderstorms during this \nafternoon and evening. Mean cloud layer winds look to be \noriented nearly parallel to the low-level jet through 09-12 UTC \nWednesday and with freezing heights 12 - 13kft and tall skinny \nCAPE soundings, efficient warm rain processes capable of \nproducing heavy downpours will be at play. The unidirectional \nflow also suggests training convection or heavy rain repeatedly \nimpacting a given area, raising concerns for flash flooding. The\noverall dynamics with this event are quite impressive for July \nwith the NAEFS showing height anomalies 3 to 4 standard \ndeviations below normal as the parent trough closes off into \nWednesday with integrated water vapor transport 3-4 standard \ndeviations above normal due to the high V-wind component \nanomalies and PWATs.\n\nLatest HREFs suggest 3-hour probs for >1\" rainfall as high as \n70-90% for areas near and west of the Hudson River between \n21Z/Tue-00Z/Wed, and 30-70% elsewhere. Similar 3-hour probs for \n>3\" rainfall are 10-30% across the SE Catskills/mid Hudson \nValley after 18Z/Tue, and including NW CT after 03Z/Wed, \nhighlighting areas with greatest chances of heaviest rainfall.\n\nIt appears that the axis of heaviest rainfall will initially \nset up for areas near and west of the Hudson River this \nafternoon/early evening, before shifting east toward the \nsouthern Taconics, Berkshires and Litchfield County later this \nevening through Wednesday morning. This axis of heavy rain with \nembedded thunderstorms may then lift north into southern VT \nduring Wednesday morning. Additional slow-moving \nshowers/thunderstorms within a developing deformation zone are \nexpected during Wednesday across the region, again producing \nlocally heavy rainfall. Heavy rainfall should finally taper off \nWednesday night, however some showers could linger into Thursday\nand even Friday, especially areas south and east of Albany.\n\nIn addition to locally heavy rainfall, will have to watch for\nisolated stronger thunderstorms this afternoon/early evening,\nespecially across portions of the mid Hudson Valley and NW CT,\nwhere 00Z/HRRR suggests MU CAPES potentially reach as high as\n1500-2000 J/kg. Can not rule out isolated strong wind gusts from\nany wet microbursts that occur in these areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLatest HEFS (Ensemble River Forecasts) still suggest only low \nto very low chances (<10%) of rivers rising above minor flood \nstage for a few points in the eastern Catskills and southern VT,\nsuch as the Williams River at Rockingham and Esopus Creek at \nCold Brook. Will have to watch trends with updated NERFC river \nforecasts today, in case the higher end of QPF (exceeding 5\") \nbecomes more likely, which would then increase the potential for\nat least a few points reaching or exceeding minor flood stage.",
